response:
To improve efficiency, you can consider using a dictionary or hash table to store all the unique words encountered so far instead of repeatedly checking if each word is already present in the list. This way, you only need to check if the new word is in the dictionary, which has a faster lookup time than checking for membership in a long array. Here's how to implement this approach:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2)
    % Initialize dictionaries for storing unique words
    dict1 = dict();
    dict2 = dict();
    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));
    % Loop through words1 and add unique words to their respective dictionaries
    for w = words1
        if ~dictHasKey(dict1, w)
            dict1(w) = true;
        end
    end
    % Loop through words2 and remove corresponding keys from dict1
    for w = words2
        if dictHasKey(dict1, w)
            delete(dict1, w);
        end
    end
    % Extract unique words from dict1
    uniqueWords = sort(keys(dict1));
end
```

In this function, `findUniqueWords`, we create two empty dictionaries (hash tables) `dict1` and `dict2`. We then iterate over each word in `words1` and add it to `dict1` as long as it's not already in there (i.e., it's a unique word). After that, we loop over `words2` and delete any keys from `dict1` that correspond to these words. Finally, we extract all the remaining keys from `dict1` (i.e., the unique words) and return them. This approach should be more memory-efficient compared to creating a long array of unique words since we don't have to keep track of every single unique word seen so far. However, it may still run out of memory for very large input texts due to the size of the dictionaries. In such cases, you might want to consider implementing a sliding window approach where you only keep track of the most recent unique words instead of all of them at once.

